{"ts": 1789778242.960394, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Both {4,38,34} at 0.901 and {4,29,38} at 0.899 are statistically indistinguishable. I'll take **{4, 38, 34}** with signs {+1, \u22121, +1}: all three sub-pairs are strong (0.667, 0.759, 0.757), so it doesn't depend on any one feature.\n\n**Calibrating the OOS prediction.** The in-sample 0.901 is an upward-biased estimate, and I should not predict it directly:\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I ran ~70 evaluations and am reporting the maximum. The winner's in-sample value is inflated by whichever noise happened to favor it. With a plateau of ~15 configs bunched in 0.86\u20130.90, the spread is roughly the noise scale, so the max sits near the top of that band by construction. This alone argues for shrinking toward the plateau mean (~0.87) and below.\n\n2. **Standard error.** At n=5000 daily obs (~20 years), the SE of a Sharpe \u2248 \u221a(1/n)\u00b7annualization \u2248 0.22 annualized. Not negligible relative to the gap between candidates \u2014 which is exactly why the top several are indistinguishable.\n\n3. **Feature-level selection.** The three features were themselves picked as top performers from 40 scanned singles, so their individual edges (0.60, 0.47, 0.46) are also optimistically biased. This compounds: the combination inherits shrinkage from both stages.\n\nThe reassuring evidence is that the sign structure is real \u2014 flipping signs collapses Sharpe to ~0.34, a swing far too large to be noise \u2014 and gains came from genuine diversification across weakly-correlated features rather than from one fragile driver. So I expect real, persistent signal, just weaker than 0.901.\n\nShrinking for both selection stages puts my central estimate near **0.70**. I'll set sd = **0.25**, covering estimation error plus the risk that OOS covariance/regime differs from in-sample."}
