{"ts": 1789778291.382763, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** features {4, 38, 34} with signs {+1, \u22121, +1} \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **0.901**. Predicted OOS: **0.70 \u00b1 0.25**.\n\n**Method.** Scanned all 40 features individually, then tested pairs and triples among the ~10 strongest. Roughly 70 evaluations total.\n\n**What I found.** No single feature was dominant \u2014 the best was f4 at 0.602. Gains came from combining weakly-correlated features: pairs reached ~0.76, triples ~0.90. This is diversification doing the work, not one powerful predictor.\n\n**Why I trust the signal.** Flipping the signs collapses Sharpe from ~0.90 to ~0.34. That swing is far too large to be noise, confirming real directional structure. I also chose {4,38,34} over the nominally similar {4,29,38} because all three of its sub-pairs are individually strong (0.667, 0.759, 0.757), so it isn't propped up by one fragile feature.\n\n**Why I predicted 0.70, not 0.901.** The in-sample number is biased upward by two compounding stages of selection:\n\n- The three features were cherry-picked as winners from 40 scanned, so their individual edges are already optimistic.\n- I'm reporting the maximum over ~70 specs. About 15 configurations sit bunched in 0.86\u20130.90 \u2014 a flat plateau whose width is roughly the noise scale (SE \u2248 0.22 at n=5000). When candidates are that tightly packed, the top one is largely whichever got the friendliest noise draw, not a genuinely better spec.\n\nThe top several candidates are statistically indistinguishable, so treating 0.901 as a real edge over 0.877 would be reading noise. Shrinking toward the plateau and discounting for both selection stages gives ~0.70, with sd 0.25 to cover estimation error and possible regime shift.\n\n**One caveat:** all evaluation was in-sample on a single 5000-day panel. I had no holdout to validate the shrinkage directly, so the 0.70 estimate rests on the selection-bias reasoning above rather than on measured out-of-sample decay."}
